1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,260
Imagine saving your company $10 million.

2
00:00:04,760 --> 00:00:05,320
That'd be nice.

3
00:00:05,460 --> 00:00:05,640
Right.

4
00:00:05,940 --> 00:00:07,560
You do all the math, you run the projections,

5
00:00:07,880 --> 00:00:10,440
and you basically prove that this highly anticipated,

6
00:00:10,640 --> 00:00:13,640
incredibly expensive new app is just,

7
00:00:13,860 --> 00:00:15,420
it's going to be an absolute disaster.

8
00:00:16,040 --> 00:00:16,740
A total train wreck.

9
00:00:16,880 --> 00:00:17,080
Yeah.

10
00:00:17,300 --> 00:00:18,920
So you stop the launch,

11
00:00:19,240 --> 00:00:22,320
you literally save the entire quarter for the company,

12
00:00:22,780 --> 00:00:24,060
and then at the end of the year,

13
00:00:24,160 --> 00:00:25,840
you sit there and watch the guy

14
00:00:25,840 --> 00:00:27,780
who designed the company's new logo

15
00:00:27,780 --> 00:00:29,460
get promoted instead of you.

16
00:00:29,460 --> 00:00:31,240
Yeah, which happens all the time.

17
00:00:31,360 --> 00:00:31,780
All the time.

18
00:00:32,120 --> 00:00:33,600
And, you know, why does that happen?

19
00:00:33,680 --> 00:00:36,300
It's because he produced a tangible artifact.

20
00:00:37,020 --> 00:00:38,500
He launched a thing.

21
00:00:38,620 --> 00:00:41,060
The system has a metric for things launched,

22
00:00:41,220 --> 00:00:43,440
but it has absolutely zero capacity

23
00:00:43,440 --> 00:00:45,340
to measure the profound value

24
00:00:45,340 --> 00:00:46,880
of the things you prevented from happening.

25
00:00:47,020 --> 00:00:48,080
It's completely blind to it.

26
00:00:48,160 --> 00:00:48,460
Exactly.

27
00:00:49,020 --> 00:00:50,380
So for today's deep dive,

28
00:00:50,480 --> 00:00:51,980
we are looking at this fascinating,

29
00:00:52,240 --> 00:00:55,820
honestly kind of dense white paper from August 2026.

30
00:00:56,320 --> 00:00:58,580
It's titled Rebel Without a Cost

31
00:00:58,580 --> 00:01:00,460
by the research group Flyxion.

32
00:01:00,760 --> 00:01:02,400
Yeah, it's a really brilliant piece of work.

33
00:01:02,740 --> 00:01:03,280
It is.

34
00:01:03,360 --> 00:01:06,940
And we are unpacking this central underlying mystery

35
00:01:06,940 --> 00:01:10,000
that governs almost all of our digital lives right now.

36
00:01:10,240 --> 00:01:12,620
Like, why is it that turning our hard work

37
00:01:12,620 --> 00:01:15,320
into a measurable token or a score

38
00:01:15,320 --> 00:01:18,940
inevitably destroys the value of the work itself?

39
00:01:18,940 --> 00:01:19,300
Right.

40
00:01:19,380 --> 00:01:20,900
And the premise Flyxion tackles here

41
00:01:20,900 --> 00:01:23,040
is just pervasive everywhere you look.

42
00:01:23,520 --> 00:01:25,860
I mean, we operate under this default assumption

43
00:01:25,860 --> 00:01:30,000
that human coordination naturally evolves

44
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:30,880
toward tokenization.

45
00:01:31,040 --> 00:01:31,260
Right.

46
00:01:31,340 --> 00:01:32,600
Like everything needs a score.

47
00:01:32,780 --> 00:01:33,060
Exactly.

48
00:01:33,200 --> 00:01:34,440
You log into a developer forum,

49
00:01:34,500 --> 00:01:35,560
you get a reputation score,

50
00:01:35,720 --> 00:01:37,860
you contribute to an open source Web3 project,

51
00:01:37,980 --> 00:01:38,840
you get a governance token.

52
00:01:39,140 --> 00:01:41,540
We just assume that wrapping human effort

53
00:01:41,540 --> 00:01:44,300
in a quantifiable financialized container

54
00:01:44,300 --> 00:01:46,680
is basically an upgrade to human memory.

55
00:01:46,680 --> 00:01:48,240
But this paper is saying,

56
00:01:48,320 --> 00:01:49,940
well, they argue it's actually the opposite, right?

57
00:01:50,020 --> 00:01:51,960
Yeah, they argue it is a catastrophic downgrade.

58
00:01:52,060 --> 00:01:52,180
Yeah.

59
00:01:52,380 --> 00:01:54,600
They present this really rigorous mathematical

60
00:01:54,600 --> 00:01:56,040
and architectural proof

61
00:01:56,040 --> 00:01:58,140
showing that the tokenized self

62
00:01:58,140 --> 00:01:59,880
is not just flawed in practice.

63
00:02:00,520 --> 00:02:02,980
It's actually logically impossible to sustain

64
00:02:02,980 --> 00:02:04,540
without causing systemic harm.

65
00:02:04,800 --> 00:02:07,080
Which completely challenges the foundation

66
00:02:07,080 --> 00:02:10,540
of nearly every decentralized autonomous organization

67
00:02:10,540 --> 00:02:13,320
or DAO operating today.

68
00:02:13,480 --> 00:02:15,740
I mean, it pulls the rug right out from under them.

69
00:02:15,740 --> 00:02:16,500
It really does.

70
00:02:16,680 --> 00:02:18,680
So let's start with the feasibility study,

71
00:02:18,860 --> 00:02:19,660
the paper critiques,

72
00:02:19,800 --> 00:02:21,480
because I think it sets the stage perfectly.

73
00:02:21,640 --> 00:02:24,740
It's referred to as the Ahorian study.

74
00:02:24,960 --> 00:02:26,020
Yeah, the IAM project.

75
00:02:26,140 --> 00:02:26,320
Right.

76
00:02:26,540 --> 00:02:28,220
And this was a real world attempt

77
00:02:28,220 --> 00:02:31,420
to solve the classic free rider problem, right?

78
00:02:31,480 --> 00:02:34,620
Like communities realize that vital maintenance work

79
00:02:34,620 --> 00:02:36,280
goes completely unrecorded.

80
00:02:36,460 --> 00:02:37,300
Right, the invisible labor.

81
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:37,940
Exactly.

82
00:02:38,400 --> 00:02:41,520
So the IAM study proposed this continuous pipeline.

83
00:02:41,920 --> 00:02:43,060
A contributor does the work,

84
00:02:43,160 --> 00:02:44,140
the system logs it,

85
00:02:44,320 --> 00:02:45,720
calculates a contribution score,

86
00:02:45,720 --> 00:02:47,540
mints a token based on that score,

87
00:02:47,720 --> 00:02:49,420
and just drops it into a wallet.

88
00:02:49,640 --> 00:02:50,620
Boom, you're rewarded.

89
00:02:50,840 --> 00:02:51,120
Boom.

90
00:02:51,740 --> 00:02:53,560
Suddenly, the person fixing the bugs

91
00:02:53,560 --> 00:02:55,420
holds a percentage of voting power

92
00:02:55,420 --> 00:02:56,840
over the entire network.

93
00:02:57,240 --> 00:02:58,820
And, you know, on its face,

94
00:02:58,860 --> 00:03:00,640
that sounds like a perfect meritocracy.

95
00:03:00,800 --> 00:03:01,240
It does.

96
00:03:01,320 --> 00:03:02,620
It sounds incredibly fair

97
00:03:02,620 --> 00:03:04,720
until you look at what's actually happening under the hood.

98
00:03:05,260 --> 00:03:07,500
The fatal flaw in the IAM system,

99
00:03:07,660 --> 00:03:08,320
according to Flyxion,

100
00:03:08,320 --> 00:03:11,200
is this phenomenon they call the Great Compression.

101
00:03:11,740 --> 00:03:12,500
The Great Compression.

102
00:03:12,660 --> 00:03:13,700
Okay, break that down for us.

103
00:03:13,780 --> 00:03:16,300
So by forcing that entire pipeline

104
00:03:16,300 --> 00:03:19,080
into a single smart contract or a single token,

105
00:03:19,680 --> 00:03:22,420
the system conflicts radically different categories

106
00:03:22,420 --> 00:03:23,300
of human judgment.

107
00:03:23,660 --> 00:03:24,860
Wait, meaning what exactly?

108
00:03:25,020 --> 00:03:27,240
Meaning you take documentary history,

109
00:03:27,620 --> 00:03:31,040
like the sheer objective fact that an event took place,

110
00:03:31,220 --> 00:03:33,180
and you crush it into economic value.

111
00:03:33,280 --> 00:03:34,180
Okay, I'm with you.

112
00:03:34,280 --> 00:03:35,740
And then you take that economic value

113
00:03:35,740 --> 00:03:38,160
and immediately crush it into political authority.

114
00:03:38,760 --> 00:03:40,460
The container holding all of this

115
00:03:40,460 --> 00:03:43,260
is just one single digital identity.

116
00:03:44,340 --> 00:03:47,780
The authors identify this as a profound category error.

117
00:03:47,900 --> 00:03:49,060
Okay, let's unpack this,

118
00:03:49,120 --> 00:03:51,660
because they introduced this chain of non-implications

119
00:03:51,660 --> 00:03:54,040
called the six acts that are not one act.

120
00:03:54,300 --> 00:03:55,740
And I really want to walk through these.

121
00:03:55,860 --> 00:03:57,020
It fundamentally breaks down

122
00:03:57,020 --> 00:03:59,900
how a healthy society actually processes information.

123
00:04:00,260 --> 00:04:02,260
Yeah, this is the core of their architectural argument.

124
00:04:02,260 --> 00:04:03,760
Right, so the sequence goes,

125
00:04:03,760 --> 00:04:07,760
record, verify, recognize, reward, authorize, and continue.

126
00:04:08,160 --> 00:04:10,420
And in our current digital infrastructure,

127
00:04:10,740 --> 00:04:13,100
these just bleed into one another constantly.

128
00:04:13,380 --> 00:04:16,200
But logically, one step does not imply the next.

129
00:04:16,360 --> 00:04:16,840
Not at all.

130
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:18,060
Let's take the first two.

131
00:04:18,920 --> 00:04:20,720
Record does not imply verify.

132
00:04:21,600 --> 00:04:23,180
Think of it like getting a receipt

133
00:04:23,180 --> 00:04:24,480
for buying a microphone on Amazon.

134
00:04:24,700 --> 00:04:25,400
That's the record.

135
00:04:25,880 --> 00:04:27,440
But that receipt doesn't verify

136
00:04:27,440 --> 00:04:29,220
that the microphone actually works.

137
00:04:29,340 --> 00:04:29,760
Exactly.

138
00:04:29,760 --> 00:04:32,860
Or if I submit a patch to a shared code base,

139
00:04:33,440 --> 00:04:36,400
the system records that I uploaded a file at 2 p.m.

140
00:04:36,660 --> 00:04:38,700
That is mere data entry.

141
00:04:38,940 --> 00:04:39,900
Right, it's just a log.

142
00:04:40,080 --> 00:04:43,220
Verification is an entirely separate evidentiary process.

143
00:04:43,920 --> 00:04:44,940
Does the code compile?

144
00:04:45,500 --> 00:04:46,940
Does it pass security checks?

145
00:04:47,360 --> 00:04:49,900
Did it actually do what the commit message claimed?

146
00:04:50,100 --> 00:04:51,360
Which is a whole different ballgame.

147
00:04:51,360 --> 00:04:54,120
And then the distinction becomes even more critical

148
00:04:54,120 --> 00:04:56,260
when we move from verify to recognize.

149
00:04:57,020 --> 00:05:00,060
Because getting a receipt isn't a five-star review.

150
00:05:00,220 --> 00:05:00,360
Right.

151
00:05:00,640 --> 00:05:02,480
Because verification is factual.

152
00:05:02,660 --> 00:05:02,880
Okay.

153
00:05:03,020 --> 00:05:04,860
But recognition is normative and social.

154
00:05:05,040 --> 00:05:06,180
Okay, give me an example of that.

155
00:05:06,220 --> 00:05:07,840
Well, we can mathematically prove

156
00:05:07,840 --> 00:05:10,140
that an individual deleted every single file

157
00:05:10,140 --> 00:05:11,060
in a shared directory.

158
00:05:11,180 --> 00:05:11,560
Oh, wow.

159
00:05:11,740 --> 00:05:12,980
Yeah, definitely verifiable.

160
00:05:13,380 --> 00:05:13,560
Right.

161
00:05:13,620 --> 00:05:16,540
The system verifies the action flawlessly.

162
00:05:16,540 --> 00:05:21,160
However, the community will absolutely not recognize

163
00:05:21,160 --> 00:05:23,280
that action as a valuable contribution.

164
00:05:24,260 --> 00:05:26,780
We frequently mistake the ability to measure an action

165
00:05:26,780 --> 00:05:29,300
for the community's endorsement of that action.

166
00:05:29,520 --> 00:05:30,760
Yeah, just because you can track it

167
00:05:30,760 --> 00:05:31,680
doesn't mean it's good.

168
00:05:32,600 --> 00:05:34,820
Which brings us to the leap that trips up

169
00:05:34,820 --> 00:05:37,580
almost every corporate environment and online community.

170
00:05:38,560 --> 00:05:40,540
Recognize does not imply reward,

171
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:44,400
and reward absolutely does not imply authorize.

172
00:05:44,400 --> 00:05:46,280
This is where the wheels really come off

173
00:05:46,280 --> 00:05:47,420
in tokenized systems.

174
00:05:47,640 --> 00:05:48,040
Exactly.

175
00:05:48,260 --> 00:05:49,900
Like, if I hire an independent contractor

176
00:05:49,900 --> 00:05:51,140
to fix a company server,

177
00:05:51,600 --> 00:05:52,880
they execute the job perfectly.

178
00:05:53,020 --> 00:05:54,780
The record is clear, the work is verified,

179
00:05:55,040 --> 00:05:56,220
I recognize their skill,

180
00:05:56,380 --> 00:05:58,100
and I reward them by paying their invoice.

181
00:05:58,340 --> 00:05:59,560
The transaction is complete.

182
00:05:59,720 --> 00:05:59,960
Right.

183
00:06:00,280 --> 00:06:02,500
But paying that invoice doesn't suddenly mean

184
00:06:02,500 --> 00:06:03,440
they should be the CEO.

185
00:06:03,880 --> 00:06:05,620
It doesn't authorize them to dictate

186
00:06:05,620 --> 00:06:06,760
the direction of the company.

187
00:06:07,440 --> 00:06:09,840
Yet, in DAOs, the moment you get a token

188
00:06:09,840 --> 00:06:10,900
for doing a job,

189
00:06:11,280 --> 00:06:12,840
you are simultaneously authorized

190
00:06:12,840 --> 00:06:14,060
to govern the protocol.

191
00:06:14,060 --> 00:06:16,140
Which is wild when you think about it,

192
00:06:16,220 --> 00:06:17,440
because historically,

193
00:06:17,880 --> 00:06:20,120
human beings have spent centuries

194
00:06:20,120 --> 00:06:21,760
constructing institutions

195
00:06:21,760 --> 00:06:24,660
specifically designed to keep these six acts

196
00:06:24,660 --> 00:06:25,860
isolated from one another.

197
00:06:26,060 --> 00:06:26,760
We have.

198
00:06:26,980 --> 00:06:27,440
Like what?

199
00:06:27,780 --> 00:06:29,060
Think about a public library.

200
00:06:29,680 --> 00:06:31,480
It's a perfect example of isolating

201
00:06:31,480 --> 00:06:33,280
the record and verify functions

202
00:06:33,280 --> 00:06:35,120
from recognition and authority.

203
00:06:35,700 --> 00:06:38,520
A library will maintain an archive of books

204
00:06:38,520 --> 00:06:40,140
promoting, you know,

205
00:06:40,200 --> 00:06:43,100
entirely discredited pseudoscientific theories.

206
00:06:43,100 --> 00:06:43,340
Right.

207
00:06:43,480 --> 00:06:45,500
You can find flat earth books in a library.

208
00:06:45,820 --> 00:06:46,120
Exactly.

209
00:06:46,580 --> 00:06:48,580
The institution preserves the historical record

210
00:06:48,580 --> 00:06:49,700
so we know it was said.

211
00:06:50,300 --> 00:06:52,080
But the presence of the book on this shelf

212
00:06:52,080 --> 00:06:54,440
is not an institutional endorsement of the theory.

213
00:06:54,880 --> 00:06:57,100
The boundaries protect the integrity of the archive.

214
00:06:57,300 --> 00:06:58,460
Oh, that makes so much sense.

215
00:06:58,560 --> 00:06:59,660
We see the same boundary management

216
00:06:59,660 --> 00:07:01,060
in professional credentials, right?

217
00:07:01,440 --> 00:07:04,160
Like, a highly specialized cardiothoracic surgeon

218
00:07:04,160 --> 00:07:06,580
wields absolute unquestioned authority

219
00:07:06,580 --> 00:07:07,960
inside an operating room.

220
00:07:07,960 --> 00:07:09,940
Literal life and death authority, yeah.

221
00:07:10,400 --> 00:07:10,540
Right.

222
00:07:10,680 --> 00:07:12,220
Their past merit dictates

223
00:07:12,220 --> 00:07:13,680
their current operational power

224
00:07:13,680 --> 00:07:15,200
in that specific context.

225
00:07:15,820 --> 00:07:16,900
But if that same surgeon

226
00:07:16,900 --> 00:07:18,260
attends a town hall meeting

227
00:07:18,260 --> 00:07:19,620
to debate zoning laws

228
00:07:19,620 --> 00:07:20,840
for a new commercial development,

229
00:07:21,360 --> 00:07:22,680
their medical credentials

230
00:07:22,680 --> 00:07:24,620
do not grant them an extra hundred votes.

231
00:07:24,740 --> 00:07:24,880
Right.

232
00:07:25,160 --> 00:07:26,480
Because their past merit

233
00:07:26,480 --> 00:07:27,620
does not translate

234
00:07:27,620 --> 00:07:29,940
into universal general authority.

235
00:07:30,160 --> 00:07:32,060
And so the fundamental danger

236
00:07:32,060 --> 00:07:33,540
of a tokenized system

237
00:07:33,540 --> 00:07:35,800
is that it mints a single asset

238
00:07:35,800 --> 00:07:37,840
that just travels effortlessly

239
00:07:37,840 --> 00:07:39,840
across all six of these acts.

240
00:07:40,060 --> 00:07:41,820
It completely obliterates

241
00:07:41,820 --> 00:07:42,960
the contextual boundaries

242
00:07:42,960 --> 00:07:45,060
that make functional governance possible

243
00:07:45,060 --> 00:07:46,100
in the real world.

244
00:07:46,300 --> 00:07:47,340
Yeah, the authors argue

245
00:07:47,340 --> 00:07:48,960
that our architectural imperative

246
00:07:48,960 --> 00:07:49,920
should not be

247
00:07:49,920 --> 00:07:52,760
how do we extract financial value

248
00:07:52,760 --> 00:07:53,880
from every human action?

249
00:07:54,460 --> 00:07:54,840
It should be

250
00:07:54,840 --> 00:07:56,380
how do we prevent the nuance

251
00:07:56,380 --> 00:07:57,340
of human history

252
00:07:57,340 --> 00:07:58,760
from being erased

253
00:07:58,760 --> 00:08:00,520
by the mechanisms of finance?

254
00:08:00,820 --> 00:08:02,260
I can hear a systems engineer

255
00:08:02,260 --> 00:08:03,320
listening to this right now

256
00:08:03,320 --> 00:08:03,700
and arguing,

257
00:08:03,840 --> 00:08:03,960
you know,

258
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:05,640
the problem isn't the token itself.

259
00:08:06,100 --> 00:08:06,940
The problem is just

260
00:08:06,940 --> 00:08:08,260
the crudeness of the algorithm.

261
00:08:08,440 --> 00:08:08,780
Oh, yeah.

262
00:08:08,820 --> 00:08:09,420
The classic

263
00:08:09,420 --> 00:08:11,500
we just need better math argument.

264
00:08:12,120 --> 00:08:12,600
Exactly.

265
00:08:12,740 --> 00:08:13,880
The pushback is always,

266
00:08:14,060 --> 00:08:15,200
well, if the token

267
00:08:15,200 --> 00:08:16,400
is creating bad governance,

268
00:08:16,400 --> 00:08:16,980
we just need

269
00:08:16,980 --> 00:08:17,740
a more sophisticated

270
00:08:17,740 --> 00:08:18,840
reputation score.

271
00:08:19,240 --> 00:08:19,900
We need to weigh

272
00:08:19,900 --> 00:08:21,120
different actions differently

273
00:08:21,120 --> 00:08:22,660
and create a perfectly

274
00:08:22,660 --> 00:08:23,900
accurate leaderboard.

275
00:08:24,380 --> 00:08:25,640
But Flyxion anticipates

276
00:08:25,640 --> 00:08:26,600
that exact argument.

277
00:08:27,380 --> 00:08:28,740
They completely dismantle it

278
00:08:28,740 --> 00:08:30,500
using order-theoretic mathematics.

279
00:08:31,340 --> 00:08:31,780
Specifically,

280
00:08:32,160 --> 00:08:32,900
they look at

281
00:08:32,900 --> 00:08:34,120
partially ordered sets

282
00:08:34,120 --> 00:08:35,040
or plus-its.

283
00:08:35,580 --> 00:08:36,560
They introduce this thing

284
00:08:36,560 --> 00:08:37,940
called Proposition 1,

285
00:08:38,480 --> 00:08:39,660
the non-canonicity

286
00:08:39,660 --> 00:08:41,160
of total contribution rankings.

287
00:08:41,160 --> 00:08:41,640
Okay.

288
00:08:41,780 --> 00:08:42,720
We're definitely going to need

