00:15:02,840 --> 00:15:04,160
that you are optimizing memory.

601
00:15:04,360 --> 00:15:04,760
Wow.

602
00:15:04,760 --> 00:15:06,760
And a prior art on this topic

603
00:15:06,760 --> 00:15:07,840
is extensive, right?

604
00:15:08,040 --> 00:15:10,120
We already rely on massive

605
00:15:10,120 --> 00:15:11,220
planet-scale systems

606
00:15:11,220 --> 00:15:12,660
that function explicitly

607
00:15:12,660 --> 00:15:14,420
as ledgers without value.

608
00:15:14,700 --> 00:15:15,060
We do.

609
00:15:15,580 --> 00:15:16,560
Wikipedia is perhaps

610
00:15:16,560 --> 00:15:18,280
the most robust example of this.

611
00:15:18,600 --> 00:15:19,640
The platform maintains

612
00:15:19,640 --> 00:15:21,880
an exhaustive page history mechanism.

613
00:15:22,440 --> 00:15:23,600
Every single keystroke,

614
00:15:23,760 --> 00:15:25,180
every addition and deletion

615
00:15:25,180 --> 00:15:26,320
by every contributor

616
00:15:26,320 --> 00:15:27,780
is immutably recorded

617
00:15:27,780 --> 00:15:29,380
and publicly verifiable.

618
00:15:29,700 --> 00:15:31,080
But the Wikipedia architecture

619
00:15:31,080 --> 00:15:32,500
strictly separates

620
00:15:32,500 --> 00:15:33,820
that documentary record

621
00:15:33,820 --> 00:15:35,320
from operational authority.

622
00:15:35,640 --> 00:15:35,720
Right.

623
00:15:35,760 --> 00:15:36,940
Like, if I spend six months

624
00:15:36,940 --> 00:15:39,280
fixing 10,000 punctuation errors

625
00:15:39,280 --> 00:15:40,820
across historical articles,

626
00:15:41,060 --> 00:15:43,100
my edit count will be massive.

627
00:15:43,360 --> 00:15:43,660
Huge.

628
00:15:43,780 --> 00:15:45,340
The system records it perfectly.

629
00:15:45,860 --> 00:15:47,100
But that high edit count

630
00:15:47,100 --> 00:15:48,720
does not automatically trigger

631
00:15:48,720 --> 00:15:49,600
a smart contract

632
00:15:49,600 --> 00:15:50,480
that promotes me

633
00:15:50,480 --> 00:15:51,860
to a Wikipedia administrator.

634
00:15:51,960 --> 00:15:53,380
No, because admin status

635
00:15:53,380 --> 00:15:55,080
is an operational role.

636
00:15:55,080 --> 00:15:57,160
It requires a completely separate

637
00:15:57,160 --> 00:15:58,780
deliberative social process.

638
00:15:59,700 --> 00:16:00,820
The community must review

639
00:16:00,820 --> 00:16:01,980
the nature of your edits,

640
00:16:02,140 --> 00:16:03,160
your communication style

641
00:16:03,160 --> 00:16:03,980
on the talk pages,

642
00:16:04,400 --> 00:16:05,000
your understanding

643
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:06,320
of consensus policies.

644
00:16:06,440 --> 00:16:07,240
It's not automated.

645
00:16:07,380 --> 00:16:08,240
And the same separation

646
00:16:08,240 --> 00:16:09,540
exists in Git too, right?

647
00:16:09,660 --> 00:16:11,040
The version control architecture

648
00:16:11,040 --> 00:16:12,260
underlying modern

649
00:16:12,260 --> 00:16:13,100
software engineering.

650
00:16:13,220 --> 00:16:13,340
Right.

651
00:16:13,480 --> 00:16:14,800
Git is a content address

652
00:16:14,800 --> 00:16:15,600
tracking system.

653
00:16:15,920 --> 00:16:17,200
It cryptographically secured

654
00:16:17,200 --> 00:16:18,100
every commit you make.

655
00:16:18,520 --> 00:16:19,540
The documentary record

656
00:16:19,540 --> 00:16:20,240
is pristine.

657
00:16:20,540 --> 00:16:21,840
But no matter how many lines

658
00:16:21,840 --> 00:16:22,680
of code I push

659
00:16:22,680 --> 00:16:23,680
to my local branch,

660
00:16:24,160 --> 00:16:25,200
that raw metric

661
00:16:25,200 --> 00:16:26,240
does not grant me

662
00:16:26,240 --> 00:16:26,900
the authorization

663
00:16:26,900 --> 00:16:28,100
to merge my code

664
00:16:28,100 --> 00:16:29,560
into the master repository

665
00:16:29,560 --> 00:16:31,360
of, say, the Linux kernel.

666
00:16:32,340 --> 00:16:32,920
Right access

667
00:16:32,920 --> 00:16:34,900
is an entirely distinct,

668
00:16:35,340 --> 00:16:37,020
manually governed layer.

669
00:16:37,260 --> 00:16:37,720
Exactly.

670
00:16:38,260 --> 00:16:39,180
And we can also look

671
00:16:39,180 --> 00:16:40,140
at the systemic failures

672
00:16:40,140 --> 00:16:40,700
that occur

673
00:16:40,700 --> 00:16:41,900
when this separation

674
00:16:41,900 --> 00:16:42,560
is breached.

675
00:16:43,100 --> 00:16:44,640
The academic publishing industry

676
00:16:44,640 --> 00:16:45,760
provides a really glaring

677
00:16:45,760 --> 00:16:46,860
cautionary tale here.

678
00:16:46,900 --> 00:16:48,220
Oh, the citation metrics?

679
00:16:48,460 --> 00:16:48,720
Yes.

680
00:16:49,260 --> 00:16:49,680
Originally,

681
00:16:49,820 --> 00:16:50,660
academic citations

682
00:16:50,660 --> 00:16:51,360
functioned purely

683
00:16:51,360 --> 00:16:52,540
as a documentary record.

684
00:16:52,960 --> 00:16:53,620
It was a map

685
00:16:53,620 --> 00:16:54,740
showing how one piece

686
00:16:54,740 --> 00:16:55,720
of research engaged

687
00:16:55,720 --> 00:16:56,660
with prior literature.

688
00:16:56,660 --> 00:16:58,020
It was a web of context.

689
00:16:58,380 --> 00:16:59,220
But then universities

690
00:16:59,220 --> 00:17:00,300
and funding bodies

691
00:17:00,300 --> 00:17:02,460
demanded a quantifiable metric

692
00:17:02,460 --> 00:17:03,740
to streamline hiring

693
00:17:03,740 --> 00:17:04,980
and grant distribution.

694
00:17:05,680 --> 00:17:06,260
They introduced

695
00:17:06,260 --> 00:17:07,200
scalar scores

696
00:17:07,200 --> 00:17:08,440
like the H-index

697
00:17:08,440 --> 00:17:10,120
and the journal impact factor.

698
00:17:10,240 --> 00:17:10,860
They compressed

699
00:17:10,860 --> 00:17:12,060
the documentary record

700
00:17:12,060 --> 00:17:13,400
into a performance metric.

701
00:17:13,540 --> 00:17:13,920
Exactly.

702
00:17:14,120 --> 00:17:14,740
And the distortion

703
00:17:14,740 --> 00:17:15,780
was instantaneous.

704
00:17:16,380 --> 00:17:17,080
Goodhart's law

705
00:17:17,080 --> 00:17:18,300
takes effect immediately.

706
00:17:18,820 --> 00:17:19,620
The moment a measure

707
00:17:19,620 --> 00:17:20,360
becomes a target,

708
00:17:20,540 --> 00:17:21,440
it ceases to be

709
00:17:21,440 --> 00:17:21,980
a good measure.

710
00:17:22,360 --> 00:17:22,680
Right.

711
00:17:23,040 --> 00:17:24,400
The metrics reform literature

712
00:17:24,400 --> 00:17:26,040
documents how researchers

713
00:17:26,040 --> 00:17:27,160
began optimizing

714
00:17:27,160 --> 00:17:28,080
for the score

715
00:17:28,080 --> 00:17:29,580
rather than the science.

716
00:17:30,360 --> 00:17:31,120
We saw the rise

717
00:17:31,120 --> 00:17:32,440
of citation cartels

718
00:17:32,440 --> 00:17:33,440
where academics

719
00:17:33,440 --> 00:17:34,780
artificially inflate

720
00:17:34,780 --> 00:17:35,600
each other's metrics.

721
00:17:35,740 --> 00:17:36,880
Which is just wild!

722
00:17:37,140 --> 00:17:38,780
And we saw a severe drop

723
00:17:38,780 --> 00:17:40,040
in replication studies.

724
00:17:40,600 --> 00:17:41,680
Because replicating

725
00:17:41,680 --> 00:17:42,720
existing work

726
00:17:42,720 --> 00:17:43,640
doesn't generate

727
00:17:43,640 --> 00:17:45,480
the same age index boost

728
00:17:45,480 --> 00:17:46,680
as publishing a novel,

729
00:17:47,180 --> 00:17:47,740
even if it's

730
00:17:47,740 --> 00:17:49,820
a statistically flimsy finding.

731
00:17:49,820 --> 00:17:51,320
So the academic crisis

732
00:17:51,320 --> 00:17:52,460
perfectly mirrors

733
00:17:52,460 --> 00:17:53,640
the governance collapse

734
00:17:53,640 --> 00:17:55,540
we see in tokenized ecosystems.

735
00:17:55,760 --> 00:17:57,180
It's the exact same failure mode.

736
00:17:57,420 --> 00:17:58,460
Okay, so if we accept

737
00:17:58,460 --> 00:17:58,940
the premise

738
00:17:58,940 --> 00:18:00,440
that tokens destroy context

739
00:18:00,440 --> 00:18:02,120
and scores manufacture bias,

740
00:18:02,300 --> 00:18:03,520
we run into a massive

741
00:18:03,520 --> 00:18:04,620
architectural problem

742
00:18:04,620 --> 00:18:05,520
regarding identity.

743
00:18:05,620 --> 00:18:06,320
Right, how do you know

744
00:18:06,320 --> 00:18:06,920
who anyone is?

745
00:18:06,960 --> 00:18:08,180
Right, because in current

746
00:18:08,180 --> 00:18:09,200
Web3 paradigms,

747
00:18:09,300 --> 00:18:10,140
your identity is

748
00:18:10,140 --> 00:18:11,260
inextricably linked

749
00:18:11,260 --> 00:18:11,920
to your wallet.

750
00:18:12,200 --> 00:18:13,060
Your standing in the community

751
00:18:13,060 --> 00:18:14,140
is literally the sum

752
00:18:14,140 --> 00:18:15,280
of the assets you hold.

753
00:18:15,580 --> 00:18:16,760
If we strip away

754
00:18:16,760 --> 00:18:17,900
the tokens and the scores,

755
00:18:17,900 --> 00:18:19,560
what exactly is

756
00:18:19,560 --> 00:18:20,560
the digital persona?

757
00:18:20,760 --> 00:18:21,260
The White Paper

758
00:18:21,260 --> 00:18:22,340
introduces this framework

759
00:18:22,340 --> 00:18:23,080
called persona

760
00:18:23,080 --> 00:18:23,880
without ownership.

761
00:18:24,900 --> 00:18:25,940
The fundamental assertion

762
00:18:25,940 --> 00:18:26,760
is that identity

763
00:18:26,760 --> 00:18:28,260
is a continuity relation,

764
00:18:28,740 --> 00:18:29,980
not a property relation.

765
00:18:30,080 --> 00:18:31,100
A continuity relation.

766
00:18:31,140 --> 00:18:31,680
A persona

767
00:18:31,680 --> 00:18:33,280
is the cryptographic tether

768
00:18:33,280 --> 00:18:34,340
that binds together

769
00:18:34,340 --> 00:18:35,520
a history of actions,

770
00:18:35,960 --> 00:18:36,880
social attestations,

771
00:18:37,260 --> 00:18:38,540
and unresolved obligations.

772
00:18:39,140 --> 00:18:40,100
It is attached

773
00:18:40,100 --> 00:18:40,800
to a subject,

774
00:18:41,060 --> 00:18:41,860
but it is not

775
00:18:41,860 --> 00:18:42,780
a bag of assets

776
00:18:42,780 --> 00:18:43,780
that can be liquidated

777
00:18:43,780 --> 00:18:44,480
or transferred.

778
00:18:44,820 --> 00:18:45,740
Which necessitates

779
00:18:45,740 --> 00:18:46,560
a deep dive

780
00:18:46,560 --> 00:18:47,580
into the mechanical

781
00:18:47,580 --> 00:18:48,100
difference

782
00:18:48,100 --> 00:18:49,440
between two concepts

783
00:18:49,440 --> 00:18:50,680
that our current systems

784
00:18:50,680 --> 00:18:51,800
frequently conflate,

785
00:18:52,240 --> 00:18:53,200
and that is recovery

786
00:18:53,200 --> 00:18:54,200
and succession.

787
00:18:54,440 --> 00:18:54,620
Right.

788
00:18:55,020 --> 00:18:56,120
Because if I am operating

789
00:18:56,120 --> 00:18:57,440
a persona without ownership

790
00:18:57,440 --> 00:18:58,840
and I lose

791
00:18:58,840 --> 00:19:00,540
my cryptographic hardware keys,

792
00:19:00,760 --> 00:19:02,100
how do I regain access

793
00:19:02,100 --> 00:19:03,280
without compromising

794
00:19:03,280 --> 00:19:04,420
the non-transferable

795
00:19:04,420 --> 00:19:05,400
nature of the identity?

796
00:19:06,100 --> 00:19:06,760
Well, recovery

797
00:19:06,760 --> 00:19:07,400
is the process

798
00:19:07,400 --> 00:19:08,240
of reestablishing

799
00:19:08,240 --> 00:19:08,980
the link between

800
00:19:08,980 --> 00:19:09,860
the living subject

801
00:19:09,860 --> 00:19:11,420
and the historical persona.

802
00:19:12,220 --> 00:19:13,380
If you lose your keys,

803
00:19:13,800 --> 00:19:14,820
the system might initiate

804
00:19:14,820 --> 00:19:15,920
a recovery protocol

805
00:19:15,920 --> 00:19:17,300
involving multi-signature

806
00:19:17,300 --> 00:19:18,000
attestations

807
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:18,920
from trusted peers.

808
00:19:19,140 --> 00:19:20,100
So real people

809
00:19:20,100 --> 00:19:20,800
vouching for you?

810
00:19:20,840 --> 00:19:21,120
Yes.

811
00:19:21,660 --> 00:19:22,520
They socially verify

812
00:19:22,520 --> 00:19:23,420
that the individual

813
00:19:23,420 --> 00:19:24,800
generating the new key pair

814
00:19:24,800 --> 00:19:26,040
is the same human being

815
00:19:26,040 --> 00:19:26,560
who generated

816
00:19:26,560 --> 00:19:27,500
the previous actions.

817
00:19:27,940 --> 00:19:28,800
You are changing

818
00:19:28,800 --> 00:19:29,800
the cryptographic lock

819
00:19:29,800 --> 00:19:30,380
on the door,

820
00:19:30,580 --> 00:19:31,920
but the system is verifying

821
00:19:31,920 --> 00:19:32,960
that the same entity

822
00:19:32,960 --> 00:19:34,240
remains inside the house.

823
00:19:34,520 --> 00:19:34,900
Got it.

824
00:19:35,480 --> 00:19:36,380
And succession,

825
00:19:36,640 --> 00:19:37,300
on the other hand,

826
00:19:37,360 --> 00:19:38,100
is the transfer

827
00:19:38,100 --> 00:19:39,280
of custodial duty.

828
00:19:39,700 --> 00:19:39,900
Like,

829
00:19:39,960 --> 00:19:41,480
if I am the lead maintainer

830
00:19:41,480 --> 00:19:42,700
of an open source library

831
00:19:42,700 --> 00:19:43,980
and I decide to step down,

832
00:19:44,200 --> 00:19:45,380
I need to pass the keys

833
00:19:45,380 --> 00:19:46,220
to a new developer.

834
00:19:46,420 --> 00:19:47,140
But succession

835
00:19:47,140 --> 00:19:48,380
does not transfer you.

836
00:19:48,920 --> 00:19:49,840
When the new developer

837
00:19:49,840 --> 00:19:51,000
assumes the role,

838
00:19:51,420 --> 00:19:51,900
they inherit

839
00:19:51,900 --> 00:19:52,920
the custodial access,

840
00:19:53,360 --> 00:19:53,840
they inherit

841
00:19:53,840 --> 00:19:54,500
the current state

842
00:19:54,500 --> 00:19:55,140
of the repository,

843
00:19:55,560 --> 00:19:56,660
and the unresolved

844
00:19:56,660 --> 00:19:57,880
operational obligations.

845
00:19:58,080 --> 00:19:58,680
But they don't

846
00:19:58,680 --> 00:19:59,760
inherit my history.

847
00:19:59,920 --> 00:20:00,260
Exactly.

848
00:20:00,820 --> 00:20:01,560
They do not inherit

849
00:20:01,560 --> 00:20:02,400
your past authorship,

850
00:20:02,880 --> 00:20:03,660
they do not inherit

851
00:20:03,660 --> 00:20:04,360
the reputation

852
00:20:04,360 --> 00:20:05,560
you built over five years,

853
00:20:05,820 --> 00:20:06,880
the new maintainer

854
00:20:06,880 --> 00:20:07,620
takes the job,

855
00:20:07,980 --> 00:20:08,640
but they do not

856
00:20:08,640 --> 00:20:09,540
acquire the standing

857
00:20:09,540 --> 00:20:10,500
to masquerade

858
00:20:10,500 --> 00:20:11,700
as the historical author

859
00:20:11,700 --> 00:20:12,760
of your previous work.

860
00:20:12,880 --> 00:20:13,040
Okay,

861
00:20:13,160 --> 00:20:14,040
to make the mechanics

862
00:20:14,040 --> 00:20:14,840
of Persona

863
00:20:14,840 --> 00:20:15,700
without ownership

864
00:20:15,700 --> 00:20:16,960
painfully concrete,

865
00:20:17,500 --> 00:20:18,580
the paper details

866
00:20:18,580 --> 00:20:19,840
this hypothetical sequence

867
00:20:19,840 --> 00:20:21,000
called the P47

868
00:20:21,000 --> 00:20:22,200
worked scenario.

869
00:20:22,460 --> 00:20:23,380
I love this scenario.

870
00:20:23,520 --> 00:20:24,120
It really grounds

871
00:20:24,120 --> 00:20:24,700
the theory.

872
00:20:24,860 --> 00:20:25,020
Yeah,

873
00:20:25,080 --> 00:20:25,520
I want to spend

874
00:20:25,520 --> 00:20:26,120
some time here

875
00:20:26,120 --> 00:20:27,040
because this is where

876
00:20:27,040 --> 00:20:28,260
the theoretical architecture

877
00:20:28,260 --> 00:20:29,540
becomes a practical roadmap.

878
00:20:30,080 --> 00:20:31,240
Let's trace the life cycle

879
00:20:31,240 --> 00:20:32,360
of a single contribution.

880
00:20:33,020 --> 00:20:33,960
We have a contributor

881
00:20:33,960 --> 00:20:34,820
operating under

882
00:20:34,820 --> 00:20:36,440
the Persona P47.

883
00:20:37,020 --> 00:20:37,580
They identify

884
00:20:37,580 --> 00:20:38,360
a memory leak

885
00:20:38,360 --> 00:20:39,520
in the resource scheduler,

886
00:20:39,540 --> 00:20:40,240
of a distributed

887
00:20:40,240 --> 00:20:41,160
computing project,

888
00:20:41,560 --> 00:20:42,340
and they submit

889
00:20:42,340 --> 00:20:43,860
a complex patch.

890
00:20:44,300 --> 00:20:44,780
Step one.

891
00:20:45,060 --> 00:20:45,800
The first phase

892
00:20:45,800 --> 00:20:47,000
is purely documentary.

893
00:20:47,960 --> 00:20:49,000
The system executes

894
00:20:49,000 --> 00:20:49,760
the record function.

895
00:20:50,300 --> 00:20:51,380
It logs an immutable

896
00:20:51,380 --> 00:20:52,600
append-only event.

897
00:20:53,240 --> 00:20:54,140
Persona P47

898
00:20:54,140 --> 00:20:55,560
submitted a specific

899
00:20:55,560 --> 00:20:56,620
cryptographic hash

900
00:20:56,620 --> 00:20:57,740
representing the patch

901
00:20:57,740 --> 00:20:59,380
at a specific timestamp.

902
00:20:59,560 --> 00:21:00,500
And there's no judgment

903
00:21:00,500 --> 00:21:01,400
attached to this log.

904
00:21:01,500 --> 00:21:01,740
None.

905
00:21:02,140 --> 00:21:02,940
It's just a fact.

906
00:21:03,560 --> 00:21:04,740
Next, step two.

907
00:21:05,200 --> 00:21:05,880
The automated

908
00:21:05,880 --> 00:21:07,620
continuous integration pipeline

909
00:21:07,620 --> 00:21:08,540
runs the code.

910
00:21:08,540 --> 00:21:10,760
